# Authentication Methods Audit

## Overview

This script audits Microsoft Entra ID user authentication method data through Microsoft Graph and exports the results to Excel, CSV, and text output.

The report is designed to provide a consolidated, one-row-per-user view of authentication registration details, phone method registration, default sign-in method preference, and SMS MFA likelihood.

## What the Script Collects

For each user, the script collects:

- User principal name
- Email address
- Account enabled status
- UPN domain
- Per-user MFA state
- Sign-in mode
- User default sign-in method
- System-preferred authentication status
- MFA registered status
- MFA capable status
- Tenant SMS authentication method policy state
- Mobile phone registration status
- SMS MFA likely availability
- Security questions registration status
- All registered authentication methods
- Phone types
- Phone numbers

## Report Format

The export is intentionally structured as one row per user.

If a user has multiple phone methods, those values are combined into the following columns:

- PhoneTypes
- PhoneNumbers

This avoids duplicate user rows and keeps the report easier to filter, summarize, and review at scale.

## SMS MFA Logic

The `SmsMfaLikelyAvailable` column is a best-effort indicator.

The script marks `SmsMfaLikelyAvailable` as `True` when a mobile phone authentication method is detected for the user.

The logic is intentionally based on mobile phone registration because mobile phone is the relevant phone type for SMS-based authentication.

## Phone Method Handling

The script evaluates phone methods as follows:

- `mobile`
  - Used as the primary signal for SMS MFA likely availability
  - Included in `MobilePhoneRegistered`
  - Included in `PhoneTypes`

- `alternateMobile`
  - Included in `PhoneTypes`
  - Not used by itself to mark SMS MFA likely availability

- `office`
  - Included in `PhoneTypes`
  - Not used for SMS MFA likely availability

## Default Sign-In Method Handling

The script also collects the user's default sign-in method from sign-in preferences.

This value is reported in:

- `SignInMode`
- `UserDefaultSignInMethod`
- `SystemPreferredEnabled`

Important: `UserDefaultSignInMethod = SMS` is informational only.

It is not used by itself to mark `SmsMfaLikelyAvailable` as true because a default method value may not always prove the method is currently registered or usable.

## Tenant SMS Policy Handling

The script collects the tenant SMS authentication method policy state and reports it in:

- `TenantSmsPolicyState`

This value is informational only.

It is not used as a hard requirement for `SmsMfaLikelyAvailable` because user-level behavior may not always align cleanly with tenant-level reporting.

## Authentication Registration Report Fields

The script also includes report-based MFA fields:

- `IsMfaRegistered`
- `IsMfaCapable`

These values come from Microsoft Graph authentication method registration reporting.

They are useful context, but they are not used as the sole authority for SMS MFA availability.

## Output Behavior

If the `ImportExcel` PowerShell module is available, the script exports:

- Excel workbook
- Text file

If `ImportExcel` is not available, the script exports:

- CSV file
- Text file

## Known Limitations

This script should be treated as a reporting and audit aid, not as definitive proof of authentication behavior.

Known limitations:

- `SmsMfaLikelyAvailable` is an inferred value, not proof of a successful SMS challenge.
- Default sign-in method is reported for context but is not treated as proof that SMS is registered or usable.
- Tenant SMS policy state is reported for context but is not treated as a hard blocker.
- Authentication registration report data and live authentication method data may not always align.
- Some Microsoft Graph endpoints used by the script are beta endpoints and may change.
- Sign-in behavior can still be affected by Conditional Access, authentication method policy scope, system-preferred authentication, user state, and service-side behavior.
- Phone numbers may be masked depending on permissions and role context.
- The report should not replace sign-in log validation when proof of actual SMS usage is required.

## Recommended Interpretation

Use this report to identify:

- Users with registered authentication methods
- Users with mobile phone authentication methods
- Users likely able to use SMS MFA
- Users with default sign-in method preferences
- Users with security questions registered
- Users with multiple phone method types

Do not use this report as the only source of truth for:

- Confirming successful SMS MFA usage
- Proving Conditional Access enforcement
- Proving effective authentication method policy assignment
- Confirming exact user sign-in behavior

## Summary

The script provides a consolidated Microsoft Entra ID authentication methods audit. It combines user data, authentication method inventory, registration reporting, phone method data, sign-in preferences, and tenant SMS policy state into a single export.

The SMS MFA likely indicator is intentionally conservative and based on mobile phone registration, with default sign-in method and tenant policy state retained as supporting context only.
